"""Offline user guide bundled with the desktop application."""

TOPICS = {
    'Getting started': (
        ('Choose a workspace', 'Use Connect to robot for live telemetry. Logs & graphs and Flight reports & playback also work offline with saved files. How to is available from the main screens; F1 opens this guide from any application window.'),
        ('A first flight review', '1. Open Flight reports & playback, then select a recording or use Open… for a ZIP/CSV.\n2. Choose One joint and All readings to inspect every supported joint measurement. Scroll vertically inside the charts.\n3. Choose Compare joints, select a measurement and tick the joints to overlay.\n4. Hover while paused, click a chart or drag the timeline to inspect a time.\n5. Use Export measurements… for the full-record report.'),
        ('What this application does', 'The live viewer reads robot telemetry. Camera, tool previews and flight playback do not command robot motion. Recorded and live data are separate workspaces. The geometry is nominal and does not apply individual robot calibration.'),
    ),
    'Connection': (
        ('Connect to live telemetry', '1. Enter the robot IP address and select its model.\n2. Enter the installation name and folder if you want to load saved installation information. SSH credentials are optional for live telemetry, but required for file retrieval.\n3. Choose Connect to robot. Wait for telemetry before interpreting the displayed pose or status.\n4. Use Disconnect to stop the live stream, or Edit connection to change its settings.'),
        ('SSH identity and credentials', 'File transfers use SSH authentication separately from the live telemetry connection. Verify the robot fingerprint when prompted. A changed identity needs review before trusting it. Passwords stay in memory. Logs and retrieval windows let you enter credentials if you connected without them.'),
        ('Installation information', 'The installation file supplies saved mounting, TCP and payload information when available. Missing credentials or an unavailable file can leave that information unavailable while live telemetry continues. Confirm the installation folder/name on the robot; do not infer payload or calibration from the visual model.'),
    ),
    'Live simulator': (
        ('Navigate the robot view', 'Left-drag to rotate and right-drag to pan in both the live and recorded robot views. Use the mouse wheel to zoom. Initial framing centers the robot, then stays fixed as it moves. Reset camera clears panning and centers/fits the current pose while preserving the selected mounting reference. The sidebar lists live joint positions in degrees and TCP position/orientation in metres/radians.'),
        ('Program line and pose', 'The program bar shows the controller URScript line when provided. This is different from a PolyScope program-tree line. The last pose is retained after disconnect; check the freshness/status banner before treating it as current.'),
        ('Live versus recorded', 'The Simulator tab follows the connected robot. Flight reports replay historical telemetry with their own playhead and speed. Neither camera manipulation nor recorded playback moves the physical robot.'),
    ),
    'Status': (
        ('Read controller signals', 'Open Status in the live viewer. The summary cards show robot, safety and program state. Expand the Controller, Safety, Motion, Joints, TCP, Electrical, I/O, Tool and Payload groups to inspect their signals. Scroll the table vertically or horizontally as needed.'),
        ('Availability and freshness', 'Unsupported signals are marked unavailable. Values are the last received snapshot, so a disconnected or stale stream can still show old values. Status bits expand into their individual flags where the controller supplies them.'),
    ),
    'View options': (
        ('Coordinate frame', 'Align view to World (mounting), Robot base or Live TCP. This changes the viewing frame, not the recorded or live measurement values.'),
        ('Overlays', 'Toggle the ground grid, coordinate axes, live TCP, installation TCP, preview tool TCP, payload/centre of gravity, labels and viewport information. Hide all overlays and Show all overlays change the group together.'),
        ('Tool preview', 'Choose a preview tool, adjust its gripper opening and choose the tool working point. These are visual previews, not robot commands or a payload dynamics simulation. Live TCP alignment positions the working point while retaining the tool’s flange orientation. Scroll View options to reach all controls.'),
    ),
    'Robot catalog': (
        ('Compare robot sizes', 'On the connection screen, choose Compare robot sizes. Select a model to review the catalog’s dimensions and reference information, then use the selected model for the connection. Choose the real robot model for live viewing; flight playback uses the model identified from the recording when possible.'),
        ('Interpret the model', 'Catalog values and nominal meshes help with visualization and comparison. They do not replace the robot’s installation, calibration or application-specific limits.'),
    ),
    'Logs & graphs': (
        ('Load incident history', 'Use Open local log… for a saved log, or enter SSH connection details and the remote log path, then choose Fetch log & graph. Retrieval includes matching .bak history. Cancel stops an active job.'),
        ('Filter and compare incidents', 'Choose Joint, Incident type and Code. Enter From/To dates as YYYY-MM-DD and use Apply dates. Reset filters clears these restrictions. Incident trends offers Day/Week/Month grouping, Bar/Line/Scatter charts, Separate codes and Aggregate.'),
        ('Details and overview', 'Select a row in Incident details to read its full message; the table shows up to 500 latest matches. Joint overview uses full-log evidence independently of chart filters. Its health signals and counts are not a prediction of failure and are not normalized by operating hours.'),
        ('Save results', 'Save filtered incidents… exports the filtered events as CSV. The plot toolbar provides pan, zoom, history and image saving. Incident logs contain event history; flight recordings contain sampled motion measurements.'),
    ),
    'Backups': (
        ('Full robot-data backup', 'In the Simulator tab, choose Full backup…. The transfer workspace uses the current robot’s SSH settings. Choose a local folder to start. If credentials are missing, enter them and choose Full backup… there. The TAR includes /programs, /apps, /data/apps, log_history.txt and log_history.bak, and flight reports. Default log/flight folders are /root and /root/flightreports; configured paths are used when available. Missing sources are skipped and listed. Full-backup TAR files are saved in your selected folder without replacing existing backups. Only after a verified local save does the app remove its temporary TAR and directory from the robot. Cancel stops the transfer; any incomplete robot archive is retained with its recovery path.'),
        ('Retrieve a backup', 'Open Logs & graphs, enter the robot’s SSH details and choose Make backup…. Select a local destination and monitor progress. Cancel stops the job. Keep the local archive for your normal maintenance workflow.'),
        ('What is copied', 'The backup workflow creates a temporary archive of /programs and /data/apps on the robot, downloads it, then removes its own temporary archive. Missing directories are skipped and listed in the final status. If neither exists, no archive is created. Permission and archive errors still stop the backup. Use Status details… to read and copy the full result. Original robot files are retained. A backup is different from a flight report; use flight retrieval for timestamped motion recordings.'),
    ),
    'Flight reports': (
        ('Open and switch recordings', 'Use Choose folder… beside Working folder to list all recording ZIP/CSV files in that folder, including its verified robot downloads. Reload folder finds new files. Choose a file in Flight report or use Open… for a single telemetry ZIP/CSV. Select assets/flightreports to browse supplied recordings. Changing folders clears the previous playback; downloaded reports are added automatically. Switching recordings resets playback, graphs and script context together. A failed load retains the previous report. Runtime-statistics psm.ura and robot-configuration ZIPs are not motion recordings.'),
        ('View all readings for one joint', 'Choose Mode: One joint, then Base, Shoulder, Elbow or Wrist 1–3. View: All readings shows position, velocity, acceleration, current, torque, temperature, voltage and control mode in synchronized charts. Scroll inside the charts or use Page Up/Page Down when the chart has focus. Select a single View for a larger graph.'),
        ('Timeline and script', 'Play/Pause, Restart, the speed selector and timeline slider control playback. Hover a graph while paused or click it to move the playhead; pause chart pan/zoom first. Open simulator shows full-size recorded playback in the main window. Sample details and Recording & script keep the measurements and source context separate from the graph. Script highlighting uses recorded controller script-line information, when present.'),
        ('Graphs and time', 'The horizontal axis labels show recorded script line numbers in chronological order. Repeated lines keep their place in time; missing lines and recording gaps show an em dash. A pinned line axis and current script line remain visible below the scrolling graphs. The playback clock still shows elapsed time since the first sample. The toolbar pans/zooms, moves through view history and saves an image; Reset zoom restores the full recording’s time range.'),
        ('Units and missing data', 'Position is rad, velocity rad/s, acceleration rad/s², current A, torque Nm, temperature °C and voltage V. Control mode is the recorded numeric code, shown as a step trace. Actual acceleration is derived from velocity only when no actual acceleration channel exists. Missing channels and gaps remain unavailable, never zero-filled.'),
        ('Torque provenance', 'The supplied recordings contain Target moment j0–j5 [Nm], displayed as target torque. They do not contain actual torque. Actual moment/torque channels are displayed only when explicitly recorded. Current is never converted into torque by this application. Do not interpret target torque as measured load or as a torque limit.'),
    ),
    'Compare joints': (
        ('Overlay the same measurement', '1. In Flight reports choose Mode: Compare joints.\n2. Choose View: Position, Velocity, Acceleration, Current, Torque, Temperature, Voltage or Control Mode.\n3. Tick the joints to compare; untick others to reduce clutter.\n4. Choose Series: Actual, Target or Actual + target. For the supplied recordings, choose Target or Actual + target when comparing torque.\n5. Move the playhead to read each selected joint at the same recorded sample.'),
        ('Combined or separate graphs', 'In Compare joints, choose Graphs: Combined to overlay the selected joints, or Separate joints for a graph per selected joint. Separate graphs share the time axis. Scale: Per joint makes small changes visible; Shared uses the same value scale across joints. Scroll to see all joints; changing layout preserves playback time and joint selections.'),
        ('Read the legend', 'Each joint keeps the same color when switching measurements. The legend identifies the joint colors, and the chart title shows the selected series. Solid lines are actual values and dashed lines are targets. Derived acceleration is identified in the sample readout. All lines on a comparison chart use the same unit and vertical scale. Missing selected channels are not replaced with another source.'),
        ('Return to one joint', 'Choose One joint to return to the previous single-joint View. Joint selections and playback time are retained while changing view mode. All readings keeps measurements with different units on separate axes rather than overlaying them on one scale.'),
    ),
    'Robot flight retrieval': (
        ('List and download', '1. Connect to the robot in the main window and wait for telemetry.\n2. Open Flight reports → Retrieve from robot….\n3. Confirm SSH credentials and choose Refresh reports. The default verified folder is /root/flightreports.\n4. Select rows with Ctrl/Shift, then Download selected, or use Download all new.\n5. Completed and validated reports appear in the flight selector without restarting.'),
        ('Progress and local library', 'The table shows file name, date, size and local download status. Progress shows current file and whole batch, with speed/remaining time when available. Choose folder… changes the working folder in both retrieval and graphs; the default is the application data folder in your user profile. Refresh after switching. Already downloaded means a verified copy exists in this working folder, not in a different folder. Reports are separated by verified source robot identity.'),
        ('Cancel, retry and refresh', 'Cancel transfer stops the current operation. Completed reports remain available; partial files are discarded. Retry failed restarts incomplete files. Refresh after reconnecting or changing settings. Downloads only read robot flight files and do not delete or modify them. Publication waits for size, ZIP and parser checks.'),
    ),
    'Recorded playback': (
        ('Full-size robot playback', 'Load a flight report, then choose Open simulator. The main window displays recorded motion at full size. In the Simulator tab, use Motion source to switch between Live robot and Flight report. Live telemetry and recorded poses stay separate. Returning to Live robot pauses playback and retains its position. Without a connection, the live view offers Connection setup.'),
        ('Controls', 'Use Play/Pause, Restart, speed and the timeline in either window. Flight charts returns to the charts without resetting time. Scrubbing a chart or timeline updates the same playhead. Closing the flight window pauses playback; returning to connection setup also pauses it.'),
        ('Mounting alignment', 'Recorded playback defaults to TCP mounting. Use Align view to in its toolbar to switch between TCP mounting and Robot base. TCP mounting anchors the view to the recorded TCP pose when selected (or the first valid sample on opening); that reference stays fixed while playback runs. This changes display alignment only. It does not change robot mounting settings or recorded measurements.'),
        ('Missing data and geometry', 'Playback uses recorded joint angles and the model identified by the recording. It does not move the robot. Gaps and missing joint angles show an unavailable state; an unknown model is not guessed. Nominal geometry is not a calibrated dynamics simulation.'),
    ),
    'Program Check': (
        ('Create the program report', '1. Open Program Check & PDF.\n2. Choose Local folder and browse your saved files, or select Robot and Pull programs… into a new local folder. Robot mode uses SSH credentials and host verification; live telemetry is not required.\n3. Select a Program (URP). Its matching script and referenced installation are loaded automatically and only that program is reviewed. Folder paths distinguish identical names.\n4. A missing installation shows a warning; the report still runs without installation settings. Export report… saves the selected report.\n5. Advanced… contains optional limits, guidance profiles and archive/manual sources. Generate report applies changed options. Cancel stops retrieval or the current review.'),
        ('Which files to use', 'Use the .urp and generated .script saved together by PolyScope, with the referenced .installation in the selected directory. The URP installation path is matched only against supplied files; if no path is declared, a unique same-folder installation is used. Missing or ambiguous matches are flagged. Saved payload/TCP values and hashes are included in the report. Automatic pairing requires the same folder and filename stem. You may explicitly select both files together if stored separately; confirm that they match. URP alone provides metadata but cannot establish the commands generated by PolyScope. A flight recording is not required.'),
        ('What the check establishes', 'The report resolves bounded arithmetic, aliases and pose variables, and reviews motion velocity/acceleration inputs against supplied specifications or selected acceleration guidance. Cartesian waypoints provide straight/circular move distances, radius-versus-distance checks and adjacent radius overlap findings. Known source distance includes only resolved segments; unknown approaches, joint-space travel, rotations and repeated execution are excluded. Runtime variables and uncertain control flow remain manual review. Source locations, file hashes and UR references are included. The program is never executed.'),
        ('Interpret results', 'Within guideline applies only to resolved source acceleration values. Review recommended identifies a source value requiring attention. Source observation records a call without proving execution. Manual review means the input cannot establish the outcome. UR deployment acceleration recommendations are starting points, not safety limits; they apply to the selected robot family. Motion version 2 can adapt blend radii, so a possible geometric overlap is not a proven skipped waypoint.'),
        ('Program report versus measurements', 'Program Check exports the source-review PDF with summary, checklist, detailed evidence and references. Flight reports → Export measurements… exports recorded telemetry separately. Use recorded measurements and logs to investigate observed runtime behavior; static checks do not certify safety, cycle time or physical suitability.'),
    ),
    'PDF and exports': (
        ('Flight PDF', 'Choose Export measurements… in Flight reports and select a destination. Export uses the complete selected recording and all six joints, independently of graph zoom, comparison mode or the selected joint. Export runs in the background.'),
        ('Separate report template', 'The flight measurement PDF keeps its separate A4 template: summary, checklist, observations, per-joint position/velocity/acceleration/current charts and action summary. Torque, temperature, voltage and control-mode views are currently available in the interactive workspace; changing the interactive view does not change the PDF template.'),
        ('Other exports', 'Use the chart toolbar’s save action to save the current chart image, including torque or joint comparisons. Logs & graphs → Save filtered incidents… exports incident CSV data. A static program-call count is source evidence, not proof that a branch ran.'),
    ),
    'Troubleshooting & shortcuts': (
        ('No connection or stale telemetry', 'Check the robot IP, network reachability and whether the controller exposes the required telemetry service. Reconnect and wait for fresh data. A retained pose or status snapshot is not a new sample. SSH failures affect file retrieval separately from telemetry.'),
        ('Empty or unavailable charts', 'Check the selected recording, View, Series and ticked joints. Supplied torque exists only as Target. Missing measurements, invalid samples and gaps are deliberately left blank. Unknown robot geometry or missing joint angles can prevent animation even when other chart channels exist.'),
        ('Transfer or export failed', 'Read the status message. Check credentials, remote folder, destination permissions and free disk space. Refresh changed files before retrying. A changed SSH fingerprint needs verification. Local imports remain available while a robot is disconnected.'),
        ('Keyboard and navigation', 'F1 opens How to. Ctrl+1 returns to the simulator/connection window; Ctrl+2 opens logs; Ctrl+3 opens flights; Ctrl+4 opens Program Check. These shortcuts pause while a confirmation dialog is open. Dialogs have Copy details and return keyboard focus to the previous control. Tab moves between controls; visible focus identifies the active control. Use arrow keys in selectors and topic lists, Space to toggle a focused checkbox, and Page Up/Page Down to scroll a focused chart or guide. Ctrl+A selects all report rows in retrieval. Escape closes the guide or cancels a confirmation. File dialogs follow the operating system’s keyboard controls.'),
    ),
}
